<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
                  xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC"
                  xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:bootstrap.processes="https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/processes/ns/"
                  xmlns:cat-harness.processes="https://litlfred.github.io/cat-harness/v0/processes/ns/"
                  id="Definitions_BeanDrain"
                  targetNamespace="https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/workflows">
  <bpmn:import namespace="https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/processes/ns/" location="https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/processes/ns.jsonld" importType="http://www.w3.org/ns/json-ld"/>
  <bpmn:resource id="session-coordinator" name="Session coordinator"/>
  <bpmn:resource id="authoring-agent" name="Authoring agent"/>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BEAN DRAIN — the delivery loop run over a queue instead of over
       one bean (bean folio-assistant-bssi; owner choice A, 2026-10-24:
       model the drain as BPMN once m8gz's OPEN and CLOSE exist).

       Precedent: epic ml9h, the 2026-10-10 three-lane drain, which the
       unlinked-beans report (https://github.com/litlfred/cat-harness-tools/pull/703) found with no
       process. This is that process.

       EACH BEAN GOES THROUGH THE SHARED OPEN AND CLOSE. Nothing here
       claims, resolves or archives: Drain_Open calls Process_BeanOpen
       and Drain_Close calls Process_BeanClose, once per bean, and
       `check:bean-calls` holds the diagram to that. The loop is an
       exclusive gateway, not a multi-instance marker: the engine routes
       gateway loops and re-enters a called process each time its call
       activity is reached again (`enterSubprocesses`), and it does not
       read loop or multi-instance characteristics.

       ONE INSTANCE, ONE BEAN. The engine binds a single bean to an
       instance and hands it to every call. Here that is the drain's own
       bean (its epic). CLOSE is followed by more work on every pass, so
       its `resolve` notes on that bean rather than completing it — the
       engine's rule, and the right one: the drain is not done when one
       bean is. The bean each pass takes is the one the drainer names
       when it runs OPEN, by hand, as `bean-drain` §2 says.

       Node ids carry a `Drain_` prefix because the engine's
       `findInModel` returns the first matching id, and OPEN and CLOSE
       use `Open_` and `Close_`.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <bpmn:process id="Process_BeanDrain" name="Drain the beans" isExecutable="false">
    <bpmn:documentation>Draining open beans with several agents at once, as part of the SDLC: build one queue grouped by owner, then take a bean, open it, work it, close it, and take the next — refilling from the deepest queue when a drainer's list runs dry, until nothing drainable is left.

You are in this process when you are asked to drain beans, when you spawn drainers, or when you pull from a shared queue next to other agents. Each bean still goes through the ordinary OPEN and CLOSE (Process_BeanOpen, Process_BeanClose); what the drain adds is the queue around them, and the rule that a bean another agent holds is skipped, not worked. The queue is advisory: it says who should take a bean, and only OPEN's claim says who did.</bpmn:documentation>
    <bpmn:extensionElements>
      <!-- STRICT, declared: the step this gates is the one a drain skips
           under time pressure — checking a bean before working it (the
           f1e1 duplicate PRs of 2026-10-24). -->
      <cat-harness.processes:policy enforcement="strict"/>
    </bpmn:extensionElements>

    <bpmn:laneSet id="LaneSet_BeanDrain">
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_QueueKeeper" name="Queue keeper" partitionElementRef="session-coordinator">
        <bpmn:documentation>Holds the queue file and nothing else: builds it from every bean copy, grouped by owner with what was left out and why, and refills a drainer whose list runs dry. It never claims — a queue entry says who should take a bean, and only the drainer's OPEN says who did. One agent can hold this lane and the drainer lane at once.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Start</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_BuildQueue</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Refill</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Refilled</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Drained</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_Drainer" name="Drainer" partitionElementRef="authoring-agent">
        <bpmn:documentation>Takes beans from its own list one at a time and runs each through OPEN, the work and CLOSE before it takes the next. Its only bean writes are the ones OPEN and CLOSE make; a bean OPEN finds is someone else's is marked skip on the queue and left alone. It asks for a refill rather than picking from another owner's list.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Next</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_More</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_TakeNext</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Open</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Claimed</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Skip</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Work</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Drain_Close</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
    </bpmn:laneSet>

    <bpmn:startEvent id="Drain_Start" name="Asked to&#10;drain beans"/>

    <bpmn:task id="Drain_BuildQueue" name="Build the queue,&#10;grouped by owner">
      <bpmn:documentation>Read every bean copy, not only the one this checkout mounts; where copies disagree the newest `updated_at` wins and the disagreement is listed. Leave out goals and epics, blocked beans, closed or draft ones, owner-held or parked ones, and beans the drainers cannot reach — each with its reason, so nothing vanishes silently. Group the rest by owner in priority order, mark beans other agents hold as skip, and write one shared queue file. The `drain-queue` Tool (bean n7x7) builds this; until it is defined, the step is the skill's.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><bootstrap.processes:skill ref="bean-coordination"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Drain_Next">
      <bpmn:documentation>A merge, not a decision: the built queue, a closed bean and a skipped one all come back here before the drainer looks at its list again.</bpmn:documentation>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>

    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Drain_More" name="A bean left&#10;on your list?">
      <bpmn:documentation>Read off the drainer's own list in the queue file. `yes` takes the next bean in priority order. `list empty` asks the queue keeper for a refill — never a bean from another owner's list. Reached after the queue is built and after each bean is closed or skipped.</bpmn:documentation>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>

    <bpmn:task id="Drain_TakeNext" name="Take the next bean">
      <bpmn:documentation>The highest-priority bean on this drainer's list that is not marked skip. Taking it is not claiming it: the queue is advisory, so the bean still goes through OPEN's check before any work, including reading code for it.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><bootstrap.processes:skill ref="bean-coordination"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:callActivity id="Drain_Open" name="Open the bean" calledElement="Process_BeanOpen">
      <bpmn:documentation>Process_BeanOpen on the bean just taken: check it, find out whose it is, and claim it — or leave it alone when another agent holds it. OPEN's check is where the drain's claim check runs (the `bean-claim-check` Tool, bean n7x7): recent commits on every bean copy and any open PR naming the bean, before the claim. The gateway after this reads which end OPEN reached.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><bootstrap.processes:skill ref="bean-coordination"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:callActivity>

    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Drain_Claimed" name="Bean claimed?">
      <bpmn:documentation>Reads which end Process_BeanOpen reached. `claimed`: the bean is in progress under this drainer and is worked next. `not yours`: another agent or a human holds it, nothing was written to it, and the drainer marks it skip and moves on rather than stopping the drain.</bpmn:documentation>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>

    <bpmn:task id="Drain_Skip" name="Mark it skip&#10;on the queue">
      <bpmn:documentation>Record on the queue file that the bean is held elsewhere, and by whom, so no other drainer takes it next. The bean itself is not touched: closing or noting on someone else's bean is how a sibling loses work it had not finished reporting.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><bootstrap.processes:skill ref="bean-coordination"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:task id="Drain_Work" name="Work the bean&#10;(PR at the first commit)">
      <bpmn:documentation>Work only the bean OPEN claimed. Open the PR at the first commit with `Closes-bean: &lt;id&gt;` in its body — the open PR is the claim every other agent can see without the bean branch — and add its link to the bean on every copy. A cause found inside the bean that is separate work becomes a new bean, checked and claimed in its own right. A bean that waits on the owner is asked through `ask-the-person`, keeps its claim while the question is open, and goes to CLOSE as blocked if it will wait long.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><bootstrap.processes:skill ref="continual-progress"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:callActivity id="Drain_Close" name="Close the bean" calledElement="Process_BeanClose">
      <bpmn:documentation>Process_BeanClose on the bean just worked: complete and archive it on evidence, scrap it with its reasons, or record its blocker and hand it back to `todo`. On every bean copy. More of the drain follows, so on the drain's own bean the engine notes rather than resolves (see the process comment); then the drainer takes the next bean.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><bootstrap.processes:skill ref="todo-manager"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:callActivity>

    <bpmn:task id="Drain_Refill" name="Refill from the&#10;deepest queue">
      <bpmn:documentation>Refresh the queue file from every bean copy — beans closed, claimed or unblocked since it was built change it — and move beans from the deepest owner's list to the drainer that ran dry. The `drain-queue` Tool (bean n7x7) rebuilds the queue; until it is defined, the step is the skill's.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><bootstrap.processes:skill ref="idle-backlog"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Drain_Refilled" name="Anything left&#10;to drain?">
      <bpmn:documentation>Answered by the refreshed queue. `refilled`: the drainer's list has beans again, and it takes the next one straight away. `queue empty`: every drainable bean is closed, held by another agent, or left out with its reason, and the drain ends.</bpmn:documentation>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>

    <bpmn:endEvent id="Drain_Drained" name="Queue drained"/>

    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_1" sourceRef="Drain_Start" targetRef="Drain_BuildQueue"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_2" sourceRef="Drain_BuildQueue" targetRef="Drain_Next"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_15" sourceRef="Drain_Next" targetRef="Drain_More"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_3" name="yes" sourceRef="Drain_More" targetRef="Drain_TakeNext"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_4" sourceRef="Drain_TakeNext" targetRef="Drain_Open"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_5" sourceRef="Drain_Open" targetRef="Drain_Claimed"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_6" name="claimed" sourceRef="Drain_Claimed" targetRef="Drain_Work"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_7" sourceRef="Drain_Work" targetRef="Drain_Close"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_8" sourceRef="Drain_Close" targetRef="Drain_Next"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_9" name="not yours" sourceRef="Drain_Claimed" targetRef="Drain_Skip"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_10" sourceRef="Drain_Skip" targetRef="Drain_Next"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_11" name="list empty" sourceRef="Drain_More" targetRef="Drain_Refill"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_12" sourceRef="Drain_Refill" targetRef="Drain_Refilled"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_13" name="refilled" sourceRef="Drain_Refilled" targetRef="Drain_TakeNext"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="BD_14" name="queue empty" sourceRef="Drain_Refilled" targetRef="Drain_Drained"/>
  </bpmn:process>

  <bpmndi:BPMNDiagram id="BPMNDiagram_BeanDrain">
    <bpmndi:BPMNPlane id="BPMNPlane_BeanDrain" bpmnElement="Process_BeanDrain">
